{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfuii2jftx342rh1iGRLn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No. Workers are invoked by HTTP requests (plus WebSockets, cron triggers, queues, and email). A Worker can't listen on an arbitrary port like 5432 for inbound raw TCP.\n\nWhat Workers can do is open **outbound** TCP connections using `connect()` from `cloudflare:sockets`. That lets a Worker talk to Postgres, and Cloudflare's **Hyperdrive** adds connection pooling for databases. So a Worker can be the client of a proxy, but not the listening end. For inbound TCP on Cloudflare you'd use **Spectrum** (mainly an Enterprise feature) or **Cloudflare Tunnel** to forward to a server you run. To my knowledge neither one hands the connection to a Worker.\n\nThis is from memory, not a fresh check, so look at Cloudflare's current docs in case inbound TCP support for Workers has been added recently."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5823,"cache_read_input_tokens":15764,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5823},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"331af9c5-ebed-417c-82b8-a87f5df308a0","uuid":"67446aba-4636-4df0-a391-7d77d2141f90","timestamp":"2026-10-11T03:13:21.391Z","request_id":"req_011Cfuii1uoWM5UsJDbzWyru"}
